.SS "int sc_io_decode (\fBsc_array_t\fP * data, \fBsc_array_t\fP * out, size_t max_original_size, void * re)"

.PP
Decode a block of base 64 encoded compressed data\&. The base 64 data must contain two arbitrary bytes after every 76 code characters and also at the end of the last line if it is short, and then a final NUL character\&. This function does not require zlib but benefits for speed\&.
.PP
This is a two-stage process: we decode the input from base 64 first\&. Then we extract the 8-byte big-endian original data size, the character 'z', and execute a zlib decompression on the remaining decoded data\&. This function detects malformed input by erroring out\&.
.PP
If we should add another format in the future, the format character may be something else than 'z', as permitted by our specification\&. To this end, we reserve the characters A-C and d-z indefinitely\&.
.PP
Any error condition is indicated by a negative return value\&. Possible causes for error are:
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
the input data string is not NUL-terminated
.IP "\(bu" 2
the first 12 characters of input do not decode properly
.IP "\(bu" 2
the input data is corrupt for decoding or decompression
.IP "\(bu" 2
the output data array has non-unit element size and the length of the output data is not divisible by the size
.IP "\(bu" 2
the output data would exceed the specified threshold
.IP "\(bu" 2
the output array is a view of insufficient length
.PP
.PP
We also error out if the data requires a compression dictionary, which would be a violation of above encode format specification\&.
.PP
The corresponding encode function is \fBsc_io_encode\fP\&. When passing an array as output, we resize it properly\&. This function cannot crash unless out of memory\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdata\fP If \fIout\fP is NULL, we work in place\&. In that case, output is written into this array after a suitable resize\&. Either way, we expect a NUL-terminated base 64 encoded string on input that has in turn been obtained by zlib compression\&. It must be in the exact format produced by \fBsc_io_encode\fP; please see documentation\&. The element size of the input array must be 1\&. 
.br
\fIout\fP If not NULL, a valid array (may be a view)\&. If NULL, the input array becomes the output\&. If the output array is a view and the output data larger than its view size, we error out\&. We expect commensurable element and data size and resize the output to fit exactly, which restores the original input passed to encoding\&. An output view array of matching size may be constructed using \fBsc_io_decode_info\fP\&. 
.br
\fImax_original_size\fP If nonzero, this is the maximal data size that we will accept after uncompression\&. If exceeded, return a negative value\&. 
.br
\fIre\fP Provided for error reporting, presently must be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, negative on malformed input data or insufficient output space\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int sc_io_decode_info (\fBsc_array_t\fP * data, size_t * original_size, char * format_char, void * re)"

.PP
Decode length and format of original input from encoded data\&. We expect at least 12 bytes of the format produced by \fBsc_io_encode\fP\&. No matter how much data has been encoded by it, this much is available\&. We decode the original data size and the character indicating the format\&.
.PP
This function does not require zlib\&. It works with any well-defined data\&.
.PP
Note that this function is not required before \fBsc_io_decode\fP\&. Calling this function on any result produced by \fBsc_io_encode\fP will succeed and report a legal format\&. This function cannot crash\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdata\fP This must be an array with element size 1\&. If it contains less than 12 code bytes we error out\&. It its first 12 bytes do not base 64 decode to 9 bytes we error out\&. We generally ignore the remaining data\&. 
.br
\fIoriginal_size\fP If not NULL and we do not error out, set to the original size as encoded in the data\&. 
.br
\fIformat_char\fP If not NULL and we do not error out, the ninth character of decoded data indicating the format\&. 
.br
\fIre\fP Provided for error reporting, presently must be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, negative value on error\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void sc_io_encode (\fBsc_array_t\fP * data, \fBsc_array_t\fP * out)"

.PP
Encode a block of arbitrary data with the default sc_io format\&. The corresponding decoder function is \fBsc_io_decode\fP\&. This function cannot crash unless out of memory\&.
.PP
Currently this function calls \fBsc_io_encode_zlib\fP with compression level Z_BEST_COMPRESSION (subject to change)\&. Without zlib configured that function works uncompressed\&.
.PP
The encoding method and input data size can be retrieved, optionally, from the encoded data by \fBsc_io_decode_info\fP\&. This function decodes the method as a character, which is 'z' for \fBsc_io_encode_zlib\fP\&. We reserve the characters A-C, d-z indefinitely\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdata\fP If \fIout\fP is NULL, we work in place\&. In this case, the array must on input have an element size of 1 byte, which is preserved\&. After reading all data from this array, it assumes the identity of the \fIout\fP argument below\&. Otherwise, this is a read-only argument that may have arbitrary element size\&. On input, all data in the array is used\&. 
.br
\fIout\fP If not NULL, a valid array of element size 1\&. It must be resizable (not a view)\&. We resize the array to the output data, which always includes a final terminating zero\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void sc_io_encode_zlib (\fBsc_array_t\fP * data, \fBsc_array_t\fP * out, int zlib_compression_level, int line_break_character)"

.PP
Encode a block of arbitrary data, compressed, into an ASCII string\&. This is a two-stage process: zlib compress and then encode to base 64\&. The output is a NUL-terminated string of printable characters\&.
.PP
We first compress the data into the zlib deflate format (RFC 1951)\&. The compressor must use no preset dictionary (this is the default)\&. If zlib is detected on configuration, we compress with the given level\&. If zlib is not detected, we write data equivalent to Z_NO_COMPRESSION\&. The status of zlib detection can be queried at compile time using #ifdef SC_HAVE_ZLIB or at run time using \fBsc_have_zlib\fP\&. Both types of result are readable by a standard zlib uncompress call\&.
.PP
Secondly, we process the input data size as an 8-byte big-endian number, then the letter 'z', and then the zlib compressed data, concatenated, with a base 64 encoder\&. We break lines after 76 code characters\&. Each line break consists of two configurable but arbitrary bytes\&. The line breaks are considered part of the output data specification\&. The last line is terminated with the same line break and then a NUL\&.
.PP
This routine can work in place or write to an output array\&. The corresponding decoder function is \fBsc_io_decode\fP\&. This function cannot crash unless out of memory\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdata\fP If \fIout\fP is NULL, we work in place\&. In this case, the array must on input have an element size of 1 byte, which is preserved\&. After reading all data from this array, it assumes the identity of the \fIout\fP argument below\&. Otherwise, this is a read-only argument that may have arbitrary element size\&. On input, all data in the array is used\&. 
.br
\fIout\fP If not NULL, a valid array of element size 1\&. It must be resizable (not a view)\&. We resize the array to the output data, which always includes a final terminating zero\&. 
.br
\fIzlib_compression_level\fP Compression level between 0 (no compression) and 9 (best compression)\&. The value -1 indicates some default level\&. 
.br
\fIline_break_character\fP This character is arbitrary and specifies the first of two line break bytes\&. The second byte is always '
.br
'\&. 
.RE
.PP

